% Vitamin D Assay Testing: the same laboratory policy in its six A/B MAC
% jurisdictions, one LCD and one Billing and Coding article per contractor
% (Phase B of the RulesRUs plan: jurisdiction as a parameter). The claim is
% for a clinical diagnostic laboratory test, CPT 82306 (vitamin D, 25 hydroxy)
% or 82652 (vitamin D, 1,25 dihydroxy), furnished to a beneficiary with a
% diagnosis code; the scenario says which contractor's jurisdiction the claim
% is in ("the jurisdiction of claim 1 is first coast" — a template offered
% here as a candidate for dmepos.le), and the program says whether the claim
% is REASONABLE AND NECESSARY there: under that contractor's LCD, with that
% contractor's ICD-10 covered-code table. "a claim is reasonable and
% necessary in a jurisdiction" answers the same question for every
% jurisdiction at once, so one scenario compares the six.
%
%   contractor    LCD      article   ICD-10 rows   HCPCS codes of the article
%   first coast   L33771   A56841    759           82306
%   cgs           L33996   A56798    183           82306
%   wps           L34658   A57484    972 (2 groups) 82306, 82652
%   noridian      L36692   A57718    379 (2 groups) 82306, 82652
%   wellpoint     L37535   A57736    565 (2 groups) 82306, 82652
%   palmetto      L39391   A59170    588 (2 groups) 82306, 82652
%
% The six tables are LOADED from the export (sources/ab/A<article>_icd10_
% covered.csv, columns code, group, article, description), never typed. Group
% 1 is the article's list for 82306 (the 25-hydroxy assay); where the
% article has a group 2 (WPS, Noridian, Wellpoint, Palmetto) it is the list
% for 82652 (the 1,25-dihydroxy assay: the LCD's "unexplained hypercalcemia
% (suspected granulomatous disease or lymphoma)", "suspected genetic
% childhood rickets" ... — the export's HCPCS line puts both codes in
% "group 1", the ICD-10 export has the two lists; the pairing is this
% model's reading). First Coast and CGS have no 82652: a claim for it is
% never reasonable and necessary in their jurisdictions.
%
% What the six LCDs share. A closed list of indications (the article's
% ICD-10 table, plus a few conditions the LCD names in prose that the table
% has no code for); screening excluded ("Vitamin D testing may not be used
% for routine screening" — CGS, WPS; "Testing may not be used for routine or
% other screening" — Noridian, Wellpoint; "Reimbursement is not allowed for
% routine screening ... in asymptomatic individuals" — Palmetto; First
% Coast's text does not state it: its closed list does the work); once a
% deficiency is shown, further testing "only to ensure adequate replacement
% has been accomplished" (CGS, WPS, Noridian, Wellpoint, Palmetto).
%
% Where they differ.
%   Frequency: First Coast — one level per 24 hours, not more than three
%   tests per year, one per year once supplemented to normal levels. WPS —
%   one per 24 hours, once a year for conditions other than rickets, vitamin
%   D deficiency, osteomalacia and aluminum bone disease, four per year for
%   those. CGS — no number; annual testing "may be appropriate" once
%   replacement is accomplished. Palmetto — the same, and "the documentation
%   must support the need for annual testing". Noridian and Wellpoint — no
%   number; a repeat test of a clinically stable beneficiary whose level is
%   between 20 and 50 ng/ml needs documentation that "clearly indicate[s]
%   the necessity of the test" (a level above 60 may be followed until it is
%   normal).
%   Indications in prose beyond the code table: CGS "low exposure to
%   sunlight" (no ICD-10 code in any table); Palmetto "Pregnant and
%   lactating women" (no pregnancy code in its table). Indications the LCD
%   restricts beyond the table: Noridian and Wellpoint cover osteoporosis
%   only "if" one of five conditions holds (a T score on a DEXA scan, a
%   history of fragility fractures, a FRAX probability, initiating
%   bisphosphonate therapy) — read here for the osteoporosis-without-
%   fracture codes M81.0, M81.6, M81.8; a code with a current pathological
%   fracture (M80.-) states condition ii by itself. The thresholds of
%   conditions i, iii and iv are lost in the export ("T score on DEXA scan",
%   the "<" signs eaten): they are read as the record's own statements
%   ("qualifying bone density score", "qualifying fracture risk assessment").
%
% How the criteria are read. The record states the diagnosis code (the
% shared template), the conditions it documents (the shared "the medical
% record of Ann documents ..."), the dates of the beneficiary's earlier
% vitamin D tests (from which the tests in the year — the 365 days — before
% the date of service are counted, and a same-day repeat is the "24 hour
% period"), whether the beneficiary has been supplemented to normal levels
% (First Coast's "supplemented to normal levels", CGS's and Palmetto's
% "adequate replacement has been accomplished"), the most recent level and
% clinical stability (Noridian, Wellpoint), and whether the record clearly
% indicates the necessity of a repeat or annual test. Not modelled: the
% ABN modifiers, bill types, places of service and NCCI edits of the
% articles; the "storage and supplement components" billed separately
% (WPS); the "3-4 months after starting replacement therapy" advice; the
% 1,25-dihydroxy daily limit of WPS beyond the 25-hydroxy one; Palmetto's
% 12-week supplementation paragraph, whose thresholds the export lost.
%
% Payment. A laboratory test is not DMEPOS: dmepos.le's payable rule asks
% for a delivery and proof of delivery, which a test has none of, so the
% cases query only "reasonable and necessary" (and the jurisdiction-aware
% form). The benefit is stated here as diagnostic laboratory tests (Social
% Security Act 1861(s)(3), Claims Processing Manual chapter 16), so that the
% shared benefit rule for a non-DME benefit applies without a home.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    % the claim: its jurisdiction (a candidate template for dmepos.le)
    the jurisdiction of *a claim* is *a contractor*; undefined.
    % the record
    *a beneficiary* had a vitamin D test on *a date*; undefined.
    *a beneficiary* has been supplemented to normal vitamin D levels; undefined.
    the most recent vitamin D level of *a beneficiary* is *a number* ng per ml; undefined.
    *a beneficiary* is clinically stable; undefined.
    the record clearly indicates the necessity of the test of *a claim*; undefined.
    % the six articles' ICD-10 tables, one per contractor
    ICD10 code *a code* is in group *a group* of article *an article* as *a description* under table first_coast_icd10.
    ICD10 code *a code* is in group *a group* of article *an article* as *a description* under table cgs_icd10.
    ICD10 code *a code* is in group *a group* of article *an article* as *a description* under table wps_icd10.
    ICD10 code *a code* is in group *a group* of article *an article* as *a description* under table noridian_icd10.
    ICD10 code *a code* is in group *a group* of article *an article* as *a description* under table wellpoint_icd10.
    ICD10 code *a code* is in group *a group* of article *an article* as *a description* under table palmetto_icd10.
    % what the LCDs and articles state
    the article of *a jurisdiction* lists HCPCS code *a code* in group *a group*.
    *a condition* is an indication in the text of the LCD of *a jurisdiction*.
    *a condition* is a qualifying osteoporosis condition of *a jurisdiction*.
    routine screening is excluded by *a jurisdiction*.
    % derived
    *a claim* is reasonable and necessary in *a jurisdiction*.
    *an item* is a vitamin D assay.
    *a beneficiary* has a covered indication for HCPCS code *a code* in *a jurisdiction*.
    ICD10 code *a code* is listed in group *a group* of the article of *a jurisdiction*.
    *a claim* is for routine screening.
    *a claim* is excluded as screening in *a jurisdiction*.
    *a beneficiary* meets the osteoporosis conditions of *a jurisdiction*.
    *a code* is an osteoporosis code.
    *a code* is a code of the deficiency conditions of wps.
    *a claim* is within the frequency limits of *a jurisdiction*.
    *a claim* is within the daily limit of *a jurisdiction*.
    *a claim* is within the yearly limit of *a jurisdiction*.
    the number of vitamin D tests of *a beneficiary* in the year before *a claim* is *a number*.
    *a claim* repeats a vitamin D test on the same day.
    *a claim* is a repeat vitamin D test.
    *a claim* is a repeat test of a stable beneficiary in the normal range.

% The six articles' ICD-10 covered-code tables, loaded from the export.
the table first_coast_icd10 is loaded from sources/ab/A56841_icd10_covered.csv, with all matches:
    code | group | article | description

the table cgs_icd10 is loaded from sources/ab/A56798_icd10_covered.csv, with all matches:
    code | group | article | description

the table wps_icd10 is loaded from sources/ab/A57484_icd10_covered.csv, with all matches:
    code | group | article | description

the table noridian_icd10 is loaded from sources/ab/A57718_icd10_covered.csv, with all matches:
    code | group | article | description

the table wellpoint_icd10 is loaded from sources/ab/A57736_icd10_covered.csv, with all matches:
    code | group | article | description

the table palmetto_icd10 is loaded from sources/ab/A59170_icd10_covered.csv, with all matches:
    code | group | article | description

the knowledge base vitamin d includes these resources:
    dmepos.

the knowledge base vitamin d includes:

